From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Laine Walker-Avina <lwalkera@ieee.org>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 22:51:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] TuyaMCU: add MCU firmware update over the Tuya upgrade
 protocol

Adds tuyaMcu_otaStart, tuyaMcu_otaAbort and tuyaMcu_otaStatus. They
update the TuyaMCU's own firmware with the standard Tuya MCU upgrade
commands: 0x0A (start, image size) and 0x0B (offset + data packets).
The image is read from LittleFS, e.g. after uploading it with
curl --data-binary @mcu.bin http://IP/api/lfs/mcu.bin.

The transfer uses the packet size the MCU asks for (256/512/1024) and
waits for each ack. It retries a packet up to 3 times and sends the
standard end marker. It then waits for the MCU's first heartbeat reply
of 0, which shows the MCU restarted. Progress is shown on the main
page and by tuyaMcu_otaStatus.

Packets are written a few bytes per quick tick, paced to the TuyaMCU
baud rate, so the UART never makes the caller wait. On Beken the quick
tick is a timer callback. Blocking writes (a 263-byte packet takes
about 0.27 s at 9600 baud) starved the web server and the task that
reloads the hardware watchdog, which restarted the module. While a
start or data packet is in progress, no other TuyaMCU frame is sent,
so frames can't interleave. TuyaMCU_SendCommandWithData drops them
with a log message. Heartbeats resume once all data is sent, so the
MCU's restart is still seen.

Enabled wherever both TuyaMCU and LittleFS are (ENABLE_TUYAMCU_OTA).
Covered by Test_TuyaMCU_OTA:
- full updates with 256- and 512-byte packets
- no other frames during a transfer, including a tuyaMcu_sendCmd
  issued mid-transfer, and heartbeats resuming afterwards
- a lost ack, a silent MCU, no answer to the start, and abort
- no restart, an unsupported packet size, and a second start while busy
- a missing file

Tested on a BK7231N with a 20 KB image at 9600 baud: about 25 s per
update. With the default 32 KB LittleFS a 20 KB image doesn't fit
next to autoexec.bat (lfs_format 0x10000 gives room). Uploads to
/api/lfs faster than about 2.7 KB/s fill the heap and trigger the
low-heap reboot, so the upload should be paced.
